<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Careers</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Want to Be a Doctor Because...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Facilitate a discussion on team roles in group work. Concept: Aid in interpreting differences in communication.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can facilitate a discussion on team roles for a career project, using gestures and visuals (e.g., pointing to each role on a poster and asking the peers about their ideas).
            Can aid in interpreting differences in communication styles by using both English and the native language, if needed, to explain career roles (e.g., using the term for "lawyer" in both languages while pointing to an image of a lawyer).
            Can clarify different job roles by miming common tasks (e.g., miming "helping" for a doctor and "teaching" for a teacher).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Teacher displays a poster with images of various professionals (doctor, lawyer, teacher, engineer, etc.). Teacher asks students, "What career do you want to pursue in the future?" and "What do you think a [doctor/lawyer] *would do* daily?" encouraging initial responses. Students practice pronunciation of minimal pairs like /ʃ/ in 'should' and /tʃ/ in 'chip' by pointing to images.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> Teacher explains a career using gestures and visuals, modeling how to express intentions and plans. "I *would like to be* a teacher because I enjoy helping others learn. I *will need to* go to university and *train* for many years." Teacher points to a picture of a teacher and mimes 'helping'. Teacher models the pronunciation of modals: 'would' /ʊ/ and 'will' /ɪ/ in sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> Teacher checks understanding of key mediation concepts.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I help my friend understand what 'qualifications' mean by using my hands, am I *facilitating* understanding?" (Yes)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If my group members don't agree on who does what for our project, and I help them decide, am I helping with *team roles*?" (Yes)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'lawyer' in English and then in Spanish to make sure everyone understands, am I *aiding in interpreting*?" (Yes)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0;">_/_/_</p>
                <p style="margin: 0;">(5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Future Careers"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue between two students, Ana and Luis, discussing their "Career Pathway Presentation" group project and how they *will plan* their team roles. The dialogue includes wh-questions about career goals, future tense for plans, and 'want to + infinitive'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Participate in group activities focused on educational and career planning, providing suggestions for improvement (e.g., "I want to be a lawyer." "I need to go take an admission test.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b><br/> Ana: "Hi Luis! For our 'Career Pathway Presentation', *what do you want to do*? *What are your goals* for our group?"<br/> Luis: "I *would like to research* 'doctor' as my career. I *will need to find* information about *qualifications* and *internships*."<br/> Ana: "Great! And I *want to prepare* the slides. Who *will practice* the presentation?"<br/> Luis: "Maybe Maria? She *wants to succeed* in public speaking. We *should ask* her."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Listen and identify: What specific careers are mentioned? (Doctor)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: Luis *wants to prepare* the slides. (False)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Who *will practice* the presentation? (Maria)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0;">_/_/_</p>
                <p style="margin: 0;">(7-9 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using wh-questions for career goals, future tense for plans ("I will need to..."), modals for intention and suggestions ("I would like to...", "We should..."), and "want to + infinitive verb" for intentions/wishes. Vocabulary focus: doctor, lawyer, teacher, career, goals, internship, qualifications, plan, practice, train, achieve, succeed, prepare, professional, skilled, important, successfully, professionally. Pronunciation: modals /ʊ/ and /ɪ/, minimal pairs /ʃ/ vs /tʃ/ (e.g., *should* vs *chip*), /dʒ/ vs /tʃ/ (e.g., *joke* vs *choke*).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice:</b> Teacher provides sentences with blanks and a word bank of target vocabulary (e.g., *qualifications, plan, achieve, professional, doctor*). Students fill in the blanks, ensuring correct grammar and vocabulary use related to career planning. E.g., "To become a _____, you *will need to* have many _____. You *should* make a _____ to _____ your *goals*." Teacher also emphasizes pronunciation of modals and minimal pairs during correction.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Substitution Drill:</b> Students work in pairs. Partner A asks a "What do you want to be?" question. Partner B responds, substituting different careers and explaining *why* using "I *want to be* a [career] because I *would like to* [verb]..." or "I *will need to* [verb] to be *professional*." E.g., A: "What do you want to be?" B: "I want to be a *lawyer* because I *would like to help* people with legal problems." A: "What do you want to be?" B: "I want to be a *teacher* because I *will need to educate* young minds."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Mediation Practice:</b> Students are given a set of scenarios where a group member might not understand a project role. They must practice explaining the role using gestures, miming, or clarifying in L1 if absolutely necessary, linking to the specific job. E.g., "What does a 'Researcher' do?" (Student mimes reading a book). "A researcher *will collect* information. Does that make sense?" Or "What is an 'internship'?" (Student explains, "It's like practice for a future career. Es una práctica profesional.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0;">_/_/_</p>
                <p style="margin: 0;">(12-15 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Future Careers"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small groups (3-4 students) continue working on their "Career Pathway Presentation." Today's focus is on *mediating* the assignment of team roles and ensuring everyone clearly understands their responsibilities for the project. Students *will use* wh-questions and future tenses to discuss and *plan* their career *goals* within the presentation task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group receives a list of typical project roles (e.g., Researcher, Presenter, Visual Designer, Editor) written on separate cards.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students in each group take turns *facilitating* a discussion about who *would be* best for each role. They ask questions like, "Who *would like to* be the Researcher for our 'doctor' presentation?" "What *will you need to do* as the Visual Designer?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If a group member is unsure about a role, other students *mediate* by explaining it using gestures (e.g., miming 'writing' for Editor), pointing to visuals (if they have career images available), or, if necessary, briefly explaining in the native language to clarify a specific term like "qualifications" or "internship" before returning to English. They must clarify different job roles by miming common tasks (e.g., miming "helping" for a doctor and "teaching" for a teacher).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The mediator for the group *will ensure* everyone understands and agrees on their assigned tasks, confirming with phrases like, "So, you *will prepare* the introduction, right?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, listening for the use of target grammar (modals, future tense, 'want to'), vocabulary, and effective mediation strategies.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0;">_/_/_</p>
                <p style="margin: 0;">(10-12 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on effective mediation (facilitation, clarification, use of gestures/L1). Each group also nominates one student to briefly *mediate* their group's role assignments to the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group's chosen mediator stands up and, using gestures or pointing to a simple written plan, explains their group's assigned roles for the "Career Pathway Presentation" to the class. They must clearly state what each member *will do* and, if needed, clarify any role using a quick mime or L1 translation for a specific word.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of modals (should, would, will) and target vocabulary (career, goals, plan, professional).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in explaining team roles and responsibilities using future tense and 'want to' statements.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of gestures, visuals (e.g., pointing), or miming to clarify.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of L1 for specific vocabulary interpretation, if necessary, followed by English.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active facilitation of discussion within the group during the performance stage.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What strategies did I use today to help my classmates understand their project roles?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident am I in explaining a job's tasks clearly to someone else?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0;">_/_/_</p>
                <p style="margin: 0;">(8-10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Self-reflection for students and teacher</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about helping my group understand their roles, and how did I overcome it?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which mediation strategies (gestures, L1, miming) did I find most useful today, and why?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific mediation strategies did students use effectively? Were they able to facilitate discussions and aid in interpreting differences in communication?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for future mediation tasks, especially regarding the use of L1 for clarification and integrating visuals more explicitly?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did students integrate the target grammar and vocabulary into their mediation efforts?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "I Want to Be a Doctor Because..." theme. The next lesson will transition to a new theme, likely building on collaborative skills for future projects or introducing new career-related vocabulary and grammar in a different context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0;">_/_/_</p>
                <p style="margin: 0;">(5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>